D={
"ayutthaya-kingdom":("U Thong (Ramathibodi I) founded Ayutthaya in 1351 on an island where three rivers meet, ideally placed for rice farming and for ships coming up the Chao Phraya. It absorbed Sukhothai, conquered Angkor in 1431, and developed the sakdina system that ranked every person by notional land rights. By the 17th century it was one of Asia's great ports, trading with China, Japan, Persia, the Dutch, French and English; the Greek adventurer Constantine Phaulkon became a royal minister under King Narai.","Ayutthaya fought Burma for two centuries; it fell once in 1569 and was restored by King Naresuan, who won a famous elephant duel in 1593. In 1767 a Burmese army of the Konbaung dynasty besieged and burned the city after a 14-month siege, destroying temples, archives and the royal family. General Taksin rallied resistance from Thonburi within months, and in 1782 Rama I founded the Chakri dynasty at Bangkok, which still reigns."),
"old-kingdom-of-egypt":("Building on the unification of Egypt around 3100 BCE, the Third Dynasty king Djoser and his architect Imhotep raised the Step Pyramid at Saqqara (c. 2670 BCE), the first monumental stone building. The Fourth Dynasty (Sneferu, Khufu, Khafre, Menkaure) built the Bent, Red and Giza pyramids, organising tens of thousands of paid labourers fed by state granaries. A powerful king ruled as a living god, supported by a vizier, scribes and provincial nomarchs.","From the Fifth and Sixth Dynasties, royal power waned as nomarchs became hereditary and wealthy, and temples won tax exemptions. Pepi II's reign of perhaps 90 years ended in succession problems. Around 2200 BCE the '4.2-kiloyear event', a global drought, brought low Nile floods and famine; Egypt split into rival centres in the First Intermediate Period (c. 2181–2055 BCE)."),
"palaiologos-dynasty":("Michael VIII Palaiologos, regent for a boy emperor of the Nicaean exile state, recaptured Constantinople from the Latin crusaders in 1261 and blinded the rightful heir to take sole power. He outmanoeuvred Charles of Anjou partly by financing the Sicilian Vespers revolt (1282). The dynasty ruled for nearly two centuries, presiding over a cultural 'Palaiologan Renaissance' of scholarship and mosaics such as those of the Chora church.","The restored empire was poor and small; civil wars in the 14th century invited Ottoman mercenaries into Europe, and the Black Death struck in 1347. By 1400 Byzantium was little more than Constantinople. Constantine XI died defending the walls when Mehmed II's cannons breached them on 29 May 1453; the last Palaiologan outposts in the Morea fell by 1460."),
"rashtrakuta-dynasty":("Dantidurga, a Chalukya feudatory, overthrew Kirtivarman II around 753 and founded the Rashtrakuta dynasty in the Deccan. His successor Krishna I commissioned the monolithic Kailasa temple at Ellora. Dhruva and Govinda III raided as far as Kannauj, and Amoghavarsha I (r. 814–878), who reigned 64 years, founded the capital Manyakheta and wrote the Kannada poetics work Kavirajamarga.","Their wars with Pratiharas, Palas, Cholas and Eastern Chalukyas were endless. Krishna III won a great victory over the Cholas at Takkolam (949), but soon after Paramara king Siyaka sacked Manyakheta (972). A feudatory, Tailapa II, overthrew the last Rashtrakuta king in 973, founding the Western Chalukyas of Kalyani."),
"zulu-kingdom":("Shaka (r. 1816–1828), a son of a minor chief, reorganised Zulu warriors into regiments (amabutho) armed with the short stabbing spear (iklwa) and large shields, using the 'bull's horns' encircling formation. In the turmoil of the mfecane he forged a kingdom of perhaps 250,000 people in what is now KwaZulu-Natal.","Shaka was assassinated by his half-brothers in 1828. Under Cetshwayo, the Zulu defeated a British army at Isandlwana (January 1879) but lost at Ulundi in July. Britain split the kingdom into 13 chiefdoms, civil war followed, and Zululand was annexed in 1887 and joined to Natal in 1897."),
"hohenzollern-prussia-and-germany":("The Hohenzollerns, electors of Brandenburg, became kings in Prussia in 1701. Frederick William I built a disciplined army, and Frederick the Great (r. 1740–86) seized Silesia and survived the Seven Years' War, making Prussia a great power. After defeat by Napoleon, reforms rebuilt it; Bismarck's wars with Denmark, Austria and France united Germany, and William I became German Emperor at Versailles in 1871.","Kaiser William II dismissed Bismarck, pursued a naval race with Britain and backed Austria in July 1914. Germany's defeat in World War I and the November Revolution of 1918 forced William to abdicate and flee to the Netherlands; the Weimar Republic replaced the monarchy."),
"house-of-wessex-and-anglo-saxon-england":("Alfred the Great (r. 871–899) of Wessex defeated the Danish Great Heathen Army at Edington (878) and built fortified towns (burhs) and a navy. His son Edward and daughter Æthelflæd, 'Lady of the Mercians', reconquered the Danelaw, and grandson Æthelstan became the first king of all England (927).","Renewed Viking attacks under Æthelred the Unready led to Danish rule under Cnut (1016–35). Edward the Confessor died childless in January 1066; Harold Godwinson defeated a Norwegian invasion at Stamford Bridge but died at Hastings against William of Normandy."),
"macedonian-dynasty-byzantine":("Basil I, a peasant-born groom who rose as a favourite of Michael III, had his patron murdered and took the throne in 867. His dynasty presided over the 'Macedonian Renaissance' in learning and art, the conversion of the Rus (988) and the reconquest of Crete, Cyprus and Antioch. Basil II 'the Bulgar-Slayer' (r. 976–1025) annexed Bulgaria.","After Basil II's death the throne passed through the daughters Zoe and Theodora and their husbands; aristocratic factions and court intrigue weakened the army. When Theodora died in 1056 the dynasty ended, and within 15 years Byzantium lost Anatolia at Manzikert and southern Italy to the Normans."),
"julio-claudian-dynasty":("Octavian defeated Antony and Cleopatra at Actium (31 BCE) and in 27 BCE took the title Augustus. He kept the forms of the Republic while controlling the army and provinces, ushering in the Pax Romana. His successors Tiberius, Caligula, Claudius (who invaded Britain in 43 CE) and Nero came from his family by adoption and marriage.","Nero's extravagance, the Great Fire of Rome (64), the persecution of Christians and revolts in Judaea and Gaul led the Senate to declare him a public enemy; he took his own life in 68. The 'Year of the Four Emperors' (69) ended with Vespasian founding the Flavian dynasty."),
"piast-and-jagiellonian-poland":("Mieszko I accepted Christianity in 966, founding the Polish state; Bolesław I was crowned king in 1025. Casimir III the Great (r. 1333–70) codified laws and founded Kraków University. Queen Jadwiga married Lithuania's Grand Duke Jogaila (Władysław II Jagiełło) in 1386, creating a union that defeated the Teutonic Knights at Grunwald (1410).","Sigismund II Augustus, last of the Jagiellonians, created the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth (Union of Lublin, 1569) but died childless in 1572, after which kings were elected by the nobility."),
"ilkhanate":("Hulagu Khan, Genghis Khan's grandson, destroyed the Assassins' fortresses and sacked Baghdad (1258), killing the last Abbasid caliph, and founded the Ilkhanate in Persia and Iraq. Ghazan Khan converted to Islam (1295), and his vizier Rashid al-Din wrote the Jami al-Tawarikh, the first world history.","The Mamluks stopped Ilkhanid expansion at Ain Jalut (1260). Abu Said died in 1335 without an heir, and the realm split into regional dynasties such as the Jalayirids and Muzaffarids, later swept up by Timur."),
"solomonic-dynasty-ethiopia":("In 1270 Yekuno Amlak overthrew the Zagwe dynasty, claiming descent from King Solomon and the Queen of Sheba as recorded in the Kebra Nagast. Emperors such as Zara Yaqob reformed the church; in the 16th century Portuguese help saved Ethiopia from Ahmad Gragn's invasion. Menelik II defeated Italy at Adwa (1896), keeping Ethiopia independent.","Haile Selassie modernised the state but faced famine, inequality and protests; the Marxist Derg military junta deposed him in 1974 and abolished the monarchy in 1975."),
"ashanti-empire":("Around 1701 Osei Tutu, with the priest Okomfo Anokye, united Akan states; tradition says the Golden Stool descended from the sky, embodying the soul of the nation. Kumasi controlled the gold and kola nut trade and the Atlantic slave trade.","Seven Anglo-Ashanti wars (1823–1900) followed; Yaa Asantewaa led the 1900 War of the Golden Stool. Britain annexed Ashanti in 1901; the Asantehene remains a traditional ruler in Ghana."),
"bourbon-dynasty-france":("Henry IV, a Protestant who converted ('Paris is worth a mass'), became king in 1589 and ended the Wars of Religion with the Edict of Nantes (1598). Louis XIII and Cardinal Richelieu centralised power; Louis XIV (r. 1643–1715) built Versailles and made France Europe's dominant power.","Costly wars, including support for the American Revolution, bankrupted the state. Louis XVI called the Estates-General in 1789; the Revolution abolished the monarchy in 1792 and executed him in 1793. Bourbons briefly returned (1814–30)."),
"romanov-dynasty":("After the 'Time of Troubles', a Zemsky Sobor elected 16-year-old Michael Romanov tsar in 1613. Peter the Great (r. 1682–1725) modernised Russia, and Catherine the Great expanded to the Black Sea.","Defeat in the Russo-Japanese War, the 1905 revolution, World War I disasters and food shortages led to the February Revolution of 1917; Nicholas II abdicated, and the Bolsheviks shot the family at Yekaterinburg in July 1918."),
"rurikid-dynasty":("Rurik, a Varangian, reportedly came to rule Novgorod in 862; his successors ruled Kiev. After the Mongol conquest, the Muscovite branch rose; Ivan III ended Mongol tribute (1480) and Ivan IV crowned himself tsar (1547).","Ivan IV killed his heir in a rage (1581); his son Fyodor I died in 1598 without children, beginning the Time of Troubles."),
"malacca-sultanate":("Parameswara, a prince from Palembang, founded Malacca around 1400 and gained Ming Chinese protection; Zheng He's fleets visited. Conversion to Islam drew Muslim merchants, and Malacca controlled the strait trade in spices and textiles.","Afonso de Albuquerque captured Malacca for Portugal in 1511; the sultan's heirs founded Johor and Perak."),
"normans-and-plantagenets":("William of Normandy won at Hastings in 1066, compiled the Domesday Book and built castles. Henry II founded the Plantagenet line (1154), ruling an 'Angevin empire' from Scotland to the Pyrenees. King John signed Magna Carta (1215).","After the Hundred Years' War, the Wars of the Roses (1455–85) between Lancaster and York ended with Richard III's death at Bosworth."),
"mamluk-sultanate-egypt":("Turkic and Circassian slave soldiers (mamluks) killed the last Ayyubid sultan in 1250. They defeated the Mongols at Ain Jalut (1260) under Baybars and expelled the last crusaders (Acre, 1291); Cairo became the Islamic world's greatest city.","Plague, Portuguese disruption of the spice trade and outdated tactics weakened them; Selim I's Ottoman artillery won at Marj Dabiq (1516) and Ridaniya (1517)."),
"tudor-dynasty":("Henry VII won at Bosworth (1485) and married Elizabeth of York to unite the rival houses. Henry VIII broke with Rome to annul his marriage (1534), dissolving monasteries; Elizabeth I's reign saw the defeat of the Spanish Armada (1588) and Shakespeare.","Elizabeth I died unmarried in 1603, and the crown passed to James VI of Scotland, beginning the Stuart line."),
"habsburg-dynasty":("Rudolf I was elected King of the Romans in 1273; marriage alliances brought Burgundy, Spain, Bohemia and Hungary. Charles V (r. 1519–56) ruled an empire 'on which the sun never set'.","Austria lost to Prussia (1866) and became Austria-Hungary (1867); the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand (1914) triggered World War I, and defeat dissolved the empire in 1918."),
"constantinian-dynasty":("Constantine won at the Milvian Bridge (312), credited a Christian vision, issued the Edict of Milan (313), called the Council of Nicaea (325) and founded Constantinople (330).","His sons fought each other; Julian 'the Apostate' tried to revive paganism and died campaigning in Persia in 363."),
"keita-dynasty-mali":("Sundiata Keita defeated the Sosso at Kirina (1235). Mansa Musa (r. 1312–1337) made his famous hajj in 1324, so lavish with gold that he depressed its price in Cairo, and built mosques in Timbuktu.","Succession disputes, Mossi and Tuareg raids and the rise of Songhai shrank Mali by the 1400s; the Keita lingered as local rulers until about 1670."),
"new-kingdom-of-egypt":("Ahmose I expelled the Hyksos around 1550 BCE. Hatshepsut, Thutmose III, Akhenaten (who tried to impose worship of the Aten) and Ramesses II built an empire into Syria and Nubia, with temples at Karnak, Luxor and Abu Simbel.","After Ramesses III fought the Sea Peoples (~1175 BCE), the Bronze Age collapse, tomb robbing, and the rise of Amun priests at Thebes split Egypt by 1070 BCE."),
"qajar-dynasty":("Agha Mohammad Khan, a Turkmen chief, reunited Iran by 1794 and made Tehran his capital.","Losses to Russia, concessions to foreigners, the 1906 Constitutional Revolution, and Reza Khan's coup (1921) led to their deposition in 1925."),
"carolingian-dynasty":("Pepin the Short deposed the last Merovingian with papal approval (751). His son Charlemagne conquered Lombards and Saxons and was crowned emperor in Rome (800), fostering the Carolingian Renaissance of learning.","Charlemagne's grandsons split the empire (Verdun, 843); Viking raids weakened them; Hugh Capet became king of France in 987."),
"kamakura-shogunate":("Minamoto no Yoritomo won the Genpei War and in 1192 was named shogun, creating rule by warriors from Kamakura; the Hōjō family later ruled as regents.","Repelling the Mongol invasions (1274, 1281) left warriors unrewarded; Emperor Go-Daigo's revolt with Ashikaga Takauji ended it in 1333."),
"safavid-dynasty":("Ismail I, head of a Sufi order, took Tabriz (1501) and imposed Shia Islam. Shah Abbas I (r. 1588–1629) moved the capital to Isfahan.","Weak shahs and the Afghan siege of Isfahan (1722); Nader Shah ended the dynasty in 1736."),
"i-vi-t-l-and-tr-n":("Lý Thái Tổ moved the capital to Thăng Long (Hanoi) in 1010 and founded the Temple of Literature (1070). The Trần (1225–1400), with General Trần Hưng Đạo, defeated three Mongol invasions, including at Bạch Đằng (1288).","Hồ Quý Ly usurped the throne in 1400; Ming China invaded in 1407."),
"pagan-kingdom":("Anawrahta united the Irrawaddy valley in 1044 and adopted Theravada Buddhism; over 10,000 temples, stupas and monasteries were built at Bagan.","Overspending on temples, tax-exempt land and Mongol invasions in 1287 broke the kingdom."),
"ptolemaic-dynasty":("Ptolemy I, Alexander's general, took Egypt in 305 BCE; Alexandria's Library and Lighthouse were built.","Cleopatra VII allied with Caesar and Antony, but defeat at Actium (31 BCE) and her suicide made Egypt a Roman province in 30 BCE."),
"hephthalites-white-huns":("Central Asian nomads who defeated the Sasanians in 484 and, under Toramana and Mihirakula, raided Gupta India.","Yashodharman defeated Mihirakula around 528; the Sasanians and Turks crushed them around 560."),
"timurid-empire":("Timur conquered from Delhi to Anatolia and made Samarkand dazzling. His grandson Ulugh Beg built an observatory.","Divided among heirs; Uzbeks took Samarkand in 1500–07. Babur founded the Mughal Empire."),
}
